1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,436
Your pepper plant isn't finished
just because the season is.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,196
A pepper is a perennial.

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:09,136
In the warmest parts of the country,
it can stay in the ground all year.

4
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:10,336
Everywhere else,

5
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:12,176
what ends it isn't age,

6
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,460
it's frost.

7
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:19,236
So before that first cold night, you can
bring one inside and keep it until spring.

8
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,356
All it takes is a pair of pruners,

9
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:22,396
a clean pot,

10
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:23,936
and fresh potting mix,

11
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:27,300
and a spot that stays around
fifty to sixty degrees.

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,800
But one step happens before
the plant ever comes through your door.

13
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,336
Skip it, and you could
carry aphids or spider

14
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,636
mites straight into your warm house.

15
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:41,016
Do it, and next spring you could start
with a plant that already has its roots,

16
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,360
not a seedling.

17
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,596
So yes, you can keep it.

18
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:47,096
There are two ways to do it,

19
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:50,256
and the one you'll pick depends
on the rooms you've already got.

20
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:51,536
We'll get to both.

21
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,240
But first, here's why it works at all.

22
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,256
A lot of us treat peppers like tomatoes.

23
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:58,736
We plant in spring,

24
00:00:58,820 --> 00:00:59,736
pick all summer,

25
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:01,216
and pull them in fall.

26
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:04,956
Honestly, it's a little sad
pulling a plant that's still green.

27
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,176
Well, two extension guides
don't see it that way.

28
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:10,436
One calls it a short-lived perennial.

29
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,416
The other calls it a tender perennial

30
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,860
that we grow as a summer
annual wherever it frosts.

31
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:17,896
That word tender,

32
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:19,316
that's the whole story.

33
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,456
It's a plant from tropical America.

34
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:24,316
Where winters stay warm,
it'll just keep going.

35
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,076
In zones nine through eleven,

36
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,636
a pepper can live outside as a perennial.

37
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:31,776
Up north, there's nothing
wrong with the plant in October.

38
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:33,680
It just can't take a freeze.

39
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:36,300
Here's why this works.

40
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,116
It's like a lamp that gets unplugged,
not a bulb that's burned out.

41
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:41,496
The bulb's still good,

42
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:43,880
it's just lost its power for the winter.

43
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:47,580
Bring the plant somewhere it won't
freeze, and you've kept the bulb.

44
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:50,576
And if you live where it never freezes,

45
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:51,496
lucky you.

46
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:53,136
You might not need any of this.

47
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:55,460
Your pepper's fine right where it is.

48
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:57,856
For everybody else,

49
00:01:57,940 --> 00:01:59,740
here's what you get for the trouble.

50
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:00,796
Come spring,

51
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:04,000
you won't be starting
with a seedling the size of your thumb.

52
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:07,696
You'll be starting with a plant
that's already got a full set of roots,

53
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:10,600
and you'll skip the nursery
bill for that plant too.

54
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:14,676
Now, here's the first thing,
and you can do it tonight.

55
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:16,396
Pick your strongest plant,

56
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:17,496
just one.

57
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:20,736
It's the one with the thickest
stems and the cleanest leaves.

58
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:21,896
The weak ones,

59
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:23,480
they'll stay in the garden.

60
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:25,496
Why only one?

61
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,396
Because one plant's easy to check,

62
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:30,456
it's easy to carry,
and it's easy to find room for.

63
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,676
A whole row of peppers in the living room?

64
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:35,276
That's how a good idea turns into a chore.

65
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,836
So you'll start with one.

66
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:39,216
If it works, you'll keep two next year.

67
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,200
That's it.

68
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:43,996
If your plants are in pots already,
you're ahead of the game.

69
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:45,456
You'll just carry the pot.

70
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:48,716
If they're in the ground,
you'll dig one up and move it into a pot.

71
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:49,956
It's worth a try.

72
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:53,580
Either way, you're working
with one plant, not a whole bed.

73
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:56,416
Now, before you pick a spot for it,

74
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:57,816
here's the odd part.

75
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:02,836
the coldest spot in your house might keep
a pepper better than your sunniest window.

76
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:04,956
I know. That sounds backward.

77
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:05,936
Stick with me,

78
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:08,280
'cause it changes where
you'll put the pot.

79
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:11,360
Here are the two ways.

80
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:13,276
The first way,

81
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:14,956
you let the plant rest.

82
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:16,416
You'll cut it back hard,

83
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:17,876
set it somewhere cool,

84
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:19,936
and water it only now and then.

85
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:22,236
It'll look like a bare stick all winter,

86
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,796
which can feel worrying,

87
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:25,056
but that's fine.

88
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:26,340
That's the point.

89
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:27,956
The second way,

90
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,216
you keep it green.

91
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,516
It stays leafy and keeps growing,

92
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,236
but it's gonna need more from you.

93
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:37,756
It'll want warmth with nights above
fifty degrees and days around seventy,

94
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:41,500
and it'll need grow lights
since a window alone won't do it.

95
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:43,516
There's a third choice,

96
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:44,796
and it's a fair one.

97
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:48,236
If you've got no room at all,
it's okay to let the plant go.

98
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,656
You'll pull it, compost it,
and plant fresh in spring.

99
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,520
Nobody's grading you on this.

100
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:55,956
So how do you pick?

101
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,796
Here's the one question to ask:

102
00:03:57,880 --> 00:04:02,376
Is there a spot in your home that stays
around fifty to sixty degrees all winter,

103
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:05,480
somewhere you walk past
but don't really live in?

104
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:07,516
Take a second and think about it.

105
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,496
Some homes have a spot
like that, and some don't.

106
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:11,700
That's okay.

107
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:13,916
If you've got that spot,

108
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,316
the resting way's probably gonna fit you.

109
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,336
It's the least work by a mile.

110
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:19,256
If you don't,

111
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:21,856
but there's a bright corner
with a plug for a light,

112
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:23,536
the green way can work.

113
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:24,796
If you've got neither,

114
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,740
the third choice makes sense.

115
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,580
Here's the side by side.

116
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:33,940
Resting needs cool and dim
and almost no water.

117
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,240
Green needs warm
and bright and regular care.

118
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,736
So the pattern's simple.

119
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:40,636
The more the plant does over winter,

120
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:42,180
the more you'll do.

121
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:44,776
And here's the thing worth knowing.

122
00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:46,056
On the resting way,

123
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:49,456
three different extension
pages land in the same place.

124
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:50,476
Cut it back,

125
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:51,716
keep it cool,

126
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,056
water it rarely.

127
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:54,576
They don't word it the same,

128
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:56,940
but those three points don't change.

129
00:04:57,880 --> 00:04:59,096
That's good news.

130
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:01,216
It means the resting way isn't some trick

131
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:03,955
that only works for one
gardener in one state.

132
00:05:04,039 --> 00:05:07,036
It's the plain,
boring method that keeps showing up.

133
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:08,640
Boring's good here.

134
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:10,636
And there's a bonus.

135
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:11,956
Once it's cut back,

136
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:13,776
a resting plant is small,

137
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:16,796
so a pot you can carry
with one hand works fine.

138
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,396
You'll be moving it twice,

139
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:20,720
once in fall and once in spring.

140
00:05:21,580 --> 00:05:24,016
One more thing before
we get to the how to.

141
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:25,696
Whichever way you choose,

142
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:29,336
that step at your door from the start
of this video still applies.

143
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:31,176
It's part of both plans.

144
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:32,380
We'll get there.

145
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:35,476
So let's start with the resting way.

146
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:36,716
It's the least work,

147
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:39,856
but it's also where a few
easy mistakes can sneak in,

148
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,320
and they start with the very first cut.

149
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:46,496
First, let's settle that
odd claim from earlier.

150
00:05:46,580 --> 00:05:49,720
Why would the coldest spot
beat the sunniest window?

151
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:53,216
Because a resting plant
barely needs light.

152
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:57,796
You'll keep it in a low light spot and
water just enough so it doesn't dry out.

153
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:00,296
That's all it's asking for. No lamp,

154
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:01,760
no sunny sill.

155
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:04,516
The green way's the one that needs light,

156
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:05,736
and a lot of it.

157
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:08,236
A winter window usually isn't enough.

158
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:09,796
So if your goal is rest,

159
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,516
a cool, dim corner isn't second best.

160
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:14,280
It's what the plant's after.

161
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:16,456
Here's why this works.

162
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:18,296
You aren't trying to grow the plant.

163
00:06:18,380 --> 00:06:22,896
You're trying to keep it alive till spring
with as little going on as possible.

164
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:24,296
You'll let it go dormant,

165
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:25,896
but you won't let it die.

166
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:28,320
Cool and dim does exactly that.

167
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:30,496
Now, the steps.

168
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:33,680
They're short, and you'll do
most of them in one afternoon.

169
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:35,336
Step one,

170
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:37,816
you'll pick every pepper
that's left on the plant.

171
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:39,900
You'll want them off before you cut.

172
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,936
Step two happens after the harvest,
but before your first frost.

173
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:47,736
You'll find the three
strongest main branches.

174
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:48,876
Everything else,

175
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:50,220
it's coming off.

176
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:51,976
Step three,

177
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:54,816
you'll strip the leaves
off those three branches,

178
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:58,176
then cut each one back
to just above the first fork.

179
00:06:58,260 --> 00:07:01,420
That's the first spot where
a branch or leaf came off.

180
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:06,036
What you've got now looks
like a short, bare letter Y,

181
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,596
maybe with a third arm.

182
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:09,176
Kind of like a slingshot.

183
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:10,236
Three stems,

184
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:11,920
and that's the whole plan.

185
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:14,676
Now, if you go looking,

186
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:17,056
you'll find the cut described two ways.

187
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:18,996
Here's where two pages differ.

188
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:20,856
A Colorado answer says

189
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:24,920
to keep three main branches
and cut just above the first fork.

190
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:26,256
A Maryland answer

191
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:31,040
says to cut the main stem six to eight
inches above the lowest side branches.

192
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,756
They're both saying the same big thing.

193
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:35,136
Cut it back hard.

194
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,856
One counts branches,
the other measures height.

195
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:41,356
A third page just says to trim
them back and keep them cool.

196
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:44,040
Pick whichever is easier
for the plant in front of you.

197
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:45,756
Step four,

198
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,996
you'll move the pot to your cool spot.

199
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:50,576
It's around fifty to sixty
degrees you're after,

200
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:52,020
if you can manage it.

201
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:53,936
Step five,

202
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:55,296
you'll water rarely,

203
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:58,160
just enough so the soil
doesn't go bone dry.

204
00:07:58,979 --> 00:08:01,136
And that's mistake number one right there.

205
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:04,136
It's watering a resting
plant like it's still July.

206
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:06,736
I get it. A bare plant looks thirsty.

207
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:09,936
But a bare plant in a cool
room isn't drinking much.

208
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:11,236
A little now and then,

209
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:12,576
and you'll let it be.

210
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:14,280
It'll thank you in spring.

211
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:16,916
You'll see something odd too.

212
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,836
Sometimes a few small leaves try
to sprout in the middle of winter.

213
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:21,876
Don't panic.

214
00:08:21,960 --> 00:08:24,236
You can just pinch them
off when they show up.

215
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:26,780
You want it asleep, not half awake.

216
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,476
So the resting plan's short.

217
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:30,496
Pick the fruit,

218
00:08:30,580 --> 00:08:32,056
cut to three stems,

219
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:33,416
strip the leaves,

220
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:35,920
then keep it cool and water rarely.

221
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:38,316
Notice what's missing, though.

222
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:42,496
We haven't brought it inside yet. That
step at the door still comes first,

223
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:44,320
and it's getting its own part.

224
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,196
That's the plant ready to rest.

225
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:48,336
Now, if your winters

226
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:51,856
are long and you'd rather look
at green leaves than bare sticks,

227
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:53,136
there's another way.

228
00:08:53,220 --> 00:08:55,100
It'll just ask more of you.

229
00:08:56,540 --> 00:09:00,096
If you've only got one bright
window and no cool room at all,

230
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:01,579
this part's for you.

231
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:06,356
The green way keeps your pepper leafy
all winter, and that's a cheerful sight.

232
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:07,476
It doesn't rest.

233
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:08,916
It just keeps on growing,

234
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:09,836
slowly.

235
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:11,396
And since it's still growing,

236
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:13,280
it'll need real conditions.

237
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:15,796
Warmth's first on the list.

238
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:18,036
Nights should stay above fifty degrees,

239
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:20,176
and days should sit around seventy.

240
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:22,856
If your house stays warm
at night, you're set.

241
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:23,916
If it doesn't,

242
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:25,720
this way's gonna be a struggle.

243
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:27,516
Then there's light,

244
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:29,300
and here's the honest part.

245
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:32,976
The guidance we found says
artificial lighting will be required.

246
00:09:33,060 --> 00:09:34,836
It doesn't say how many hours,

247
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:36,596
so we won't make up a number.

248
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:40,340
You'll use a grow light and go
by the instructions that come with it.

249
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:43,356
Why so firm on the light?

250
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:46,816
Because a plant that's still
growing is still making its own food,

251
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,856
and it makes that food from light.

252
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:52,036
A short winter day through
glass usually won't cover it.

253
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:54,760
It's like cooking dinner
over a birthday candle.

254
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:56,936
Now, the fun part.

255
00:09:57,020 --> 00:09:59,656
A green pepper plant might flower indoors.

256
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:00,596
If it does,

257
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:03,156
you'll give the plant
a gentle shake now and then.

258
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:05,356
That's a simple way to hand pollinate it.

259
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:08,816
There aren't any bees in your living
room, so you're doing their job.

260
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,240
You're the bee now.

261
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:12,916
Now, let's keep this honest.

262
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,336
Flowers are possible,

263
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:17,096
but peppers are a bonus, not a promise.

264
00:10:17,180 --> 00:10:22,096
So keep it green 'cause you like having it
around or 'cause you've got no cool spot.

265
00:10:22,180 --> 00:10:25,040
A winter harvest alone
isn't much of a reason.

266
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:29,396
And if you change your mind halfway
through the winter, that's fine.

267
00:10:29,480 --> 00:10:31,016
You can still trim it back,

268
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:32,336
move it somewhere cool,

269
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:33,616
and let it rest.

270
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:35,516
Switching to dormancy halfway,

271
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:36,720
that's allowed.

272
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:39,696
So green or resting?

273
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:41,596
Here's the quick way to think about it.

274
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:42,896
Resting's less work

275
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:44,676
with less to keep an eye on.

276
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:46,796
Green's more work and more light.

277
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:49,520
Either one can get
a healthy plant to spring.

278
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:53,716
That brings us to the job I
promised at the very start.

279
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:55,356
Whichever way you choose,

280
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,796
there's one step left
before any pot comes inside.

281
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:00,456
It's the easiest one to skip

282
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:02,780
and the one you'll be gladdest you didn't.

283
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:04,640
Here it is.

284
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:06,736
Before your pepper comes inside,

285
00:11:06,820 --> 00:11:09,356
you'll lift it out
of its pot or the ground.

286
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:12,616
Then wash every bit
of the old soil off the roots,

287
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:13,956
first with your hands,

288
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:15,240
then with water.

289
00:11:15,340 --> 00:11:20,220
Then it goes into a clean pot with fresh
potting mix that's never been used before.

290
00:11:21,380 --> 00:11:22,376
That's the step.

291
00:11:22,460 --> 00:11:25,740
It doesn't take long, and it's
the one that's easiest to skip.

292
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:28,056
Now, why bother?

293
00:11:28,140 --> 00:11:31,336
Peppers have a short list
of regular pests: aphids,

294
00:11:31,420 --> 00:11:33,276
spider mites, and whiteflies.

295
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,696
They're the usual suspects on this plant.

296
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:38,016
Nobody wants those roommates all winter.

297
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:43,080
Starting clean's a simple way to leave
the old soil and whatever's in it outside.

298
00:11:44,019 --> 00:11:45,476
Picture it for a second.

299
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:48,096
You're on the porch
with the pot in your hands.

300
00:11:48,180 --> 00:11:49,516
The door's already open,

301
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:51,656
and the house is warm behind you.

302
00:11:51,740 --> 00:11:55,196
It's so tempting to just
walk in, but stop right there.

303
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:58,980
That's where this step happens
before you've crossed that line.

304
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:03,036
While you're at it, it's worth
a close look at the plant itself.

305
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:06,676
You'll check the new growth
at the tips and the stems you kept.

306
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:10,076
You're looking for anything
that moves or anything sticky.

307
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:12,659
If it all looks clean, you're good to go.

308
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:16,036
And if you do spot aphids or spider mites,

309
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:17,036
there's a fix,

310
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:18,660
and it comes with a warning.

311
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:20,756
Before any spray,

312
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:23,256
a word for homes with pets or kids.

313
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:26,076
Got a cat, a dog, or grandkids around?

314
00:12:26,160 --> 00:12:28,376
Keep the bottle up high and out of reach

315
00:12:28,460 --> 00:12:31,936
and follow the label on when pets
and kids can be near the plant.

316
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:37,196
It's worth asking your vet about any new
plant or any spray before it comes inside.

317
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:40,376
We couldn't confirm a pet
safety listing for pepper plants,

318
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:41,776
so we won't guess.

319
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:43,636
If a child gets into the spray,

320
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:46,136
follow the first aid
directions on the label.

321
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:47,416
If a pet chews on it,

322
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:48,636
call your vet

323
00:12:48,720 --> 00:12:52,796
or call the ASPCA Animal
Poison Control line.

324
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:57,396
The number is eight eight eight
four two six four four three five.

325
00:12:57,480 --> 00:12:59,600
A consultation fee may apply.

326
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:03,396
A store-bought insecticidal
soap is one option.

327
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:04,616
Aphids and spider mites

328
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:08,676
are among the pests most likely to be
knocked back by it. You'll want one that's

329
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:13,020
labeled for the job and that label's
worth reading before you spray anything.

330
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:14,736
Here's the warning.

331
00:13:14,820 --> 00:13:18,256
Some plants are sensitive
to these sprays and can be damaged.

332
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:20,836
So it's worth a test
on a few leaves first,

333
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:23,540
then wait a bit before
you treat the whole plant.

334
00:13:24,380 --> 00:13:25,856
And that's mistake number two.

335
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:28,476
It's mixing up your own
spray from dish soap.

336
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:30,316
It sounds harmless, I know,

337
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:32,716
but one extension page says it straight.

338
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:36,456
Household soaps and cleaners
shouldn't be used as pesticides.

339
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:39,420
So it's the real thing or no spray at all.

340
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:41,976
So the doorstep's simple.

341
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:43,016
Wash the roots,

342
00:13:43,100 --> 00:13:45,216
then a fresh pot and fresh mix.

343
00:13:45,300 --> 00:13:48,596
You'll take a good look at the leaves,
and if you've got to spray,

344
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:51,380
it's a labeled soap tested first.

345
00:13:52,480 --> 00:13:56,116
Have you ever brought a plant inside
and found bugs on it a month later?

346
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:58,740
We'd love to hear how
it went in the comments.

347
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:01,336
Now, if you do all that,

348
00:14:01,420 --> 00:14:04,156
the plant has a good shot
at getting through the cold.

349
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:06,916
But there's one day that
can undo the whole winter,

350
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,820
and it's usually a warm one.

351
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:11,596
It's the first warm day of spring,

352
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:12,696
and the sun's out.

353
00:14:12,780 --> 00:14:15,276
It feels like summer is
right around the corner,

354
00:14:15,360 --> 00:14:17,896
and you'll want to carry
that pepper straight outside.

355
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:19,000
Who wouldn't?

356
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:20,996
I know the feeling,

357
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:22,920
but that's mistake number three.

358
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:25,716
A pepper is a warm season crop.

359
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:29,816
Warm season crops shouldn't go
outside until after your last frost.

360
00:14:29,900 --> 00:14:31,236
So the rule is simple.

361
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:33,476
Wait until after your last frost,

362
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:35,896
not just the first nice afternoon.

363
00:14:35,980 --> 00:14:38,920
One warm day doesn't mean
the cold nights are done.

364
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:41,596
And when's your last frost?

365
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:43,316
That depends on where you live.

366
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,916
It isn't the same
from one zone to the next.

367
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,276
So look up your own date,

368
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:50,020
not one from a neighbor three states away.

369
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:52,756
Then work backward from it.

370
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:56,136
About two weeks before
the plant moves outside for good,

371
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:58,456
you'll start getting it
used to the outdoors.

372
00:14:58,540 --> 00:14:59,456
A little sun,

373
00:14:59,540 --> 00:15:00,676
a little breeze,

374
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:03,576
then back in, and a bit more each day.

375
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:05,516
That's called hardening off.

376
00:15:05,600 --> 00:15:10,220
It's how a plant that's been indoors all
winter gets used to the real world again.

377
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:13,096
And when should you wake it up inside?

378
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:14,476
We'll be up front here.

379
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:16,936
The pages we read don't put a date on it.

380
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:18,636
So here's a way to think about it.

381
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,016
Resting meant cool,

382
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:21,396
dim, and dry.

383
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:25,736
Waking up means you'll turn those
three back around a little at a time.

384
00:15:25,820 --> 00:15:27,996
That's a brighter spot, a warmer room,

385
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:31,200
and a bit more water as
new leaves start to show.

386
00:15:32,060 --> 00:15:33,416
Picture early May,

387
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:35,716
last year's peppers sitting on the porch,

388
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:37,456
leafy and thick stemmed.

389
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:41,556
Right next to it, there's a nursery
seedling about the size of your hand.

390
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:43,056
Same kind of plant,

391
00:15:43,140 --> 00:15:44,800
very different head start.

392
00:15:45,660 --> 00:15:49,436
Now, let's be straight about
what this does and doesn't do.

393
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:51,876
This won't make a pepper
plant live forever.

394
00:15:51,960 --> 00:15:54,116
It gets one plant through one winter,

395
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:56,400
and you'll decide again next fall.

396
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:58,016
Some plants might not make it,

397
00:15:58,100 --> 00:15:59,156
and that's okay.

398
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:00,740
You'd only lose one.

399
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:03,056
So here's the whole plan,

400
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:04,556
start to finish.

401
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:09,416
You'll pick one strong plant and cut it
back to three stems before the frost.

402
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:12,176
Then you'll wash the roots
and give it a clean pot.

403
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:14,976
It stays cool and nearly dry all winter.

404
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:18,500
And after your last frost,
you'll ease it back outside.

405
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,876
Think about next summer for a second.

406
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:23,796
A pepper plant that's already big,

407
00:16:23,880 --> 00:16:26,316
already rooted, and already outside.

408
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:28,096
The seedlings are still catching up.

409
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:30,260
Not a bad way to start the season.

410
00:16:31,020 --> 00:16:34,760
You're the kind of gardener who
doesn't start from zero every spring.

411
00:16:35,580 --> 00:16:36,516
So tonight,

412
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,656
before the first frost gets here,

413
00:16:38,740 --> 00:16:40,336
pick your strongest plant,

414
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:41,296
just one.

415
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:42,396
And if you'd like,

416
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:44,960
tell us your zone
and the pepper you're keeping.

417
00:16:45,780 --> 00:16:48,176
And if you've got
geraniums in the garden too,

418
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:51,380
there's a video on saving
those before the frost.

419
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:53,356
Cut it back,

420
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:54,456
keep it cool,

421
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:55,660
bring it back.

422
00:16:56,480 --> 00:16:57,495
Happy gardening.

